  "name": "Shallow Water Fish Assemblages in the Northeastern Gulf of Alaska: Habitat Evaluation, Species Composition, Abundance, Spatial Distribution and Trophic Interaction",
  "description": "This report references diet, geographic distribution, habitat utilization, population abundance, and species composition data of fish species: Ammodytes hexapterus, Anoplarchus purpurescans, Clupea pallasi, Hexagrammos decagrammus, and Pholis laeta from Prince William Sound, Alaska. Referenced areas of Prince William Sound include: Constantine Harbor, Danger Island, Elrington Passage, Latouche Point, Little Smith Island, Naked Island, Peak Island, Schooner Rock, Zaikof Bay, and Zaikof Point. The aforementioned data are referenced in citation: Rosenthal, R. J. 1983. Shallow water fish assemblages in the northeastern Gulf of Alaska: habitat evaluation, species composition, abundance, spatial distribution and trophic interaction. In Environmental Assessment of the Alaskan Continental Shelf. Final Reports of Principal Investigators, Vol. 17, Biological studies. NOAA/NOS, Office of Oceanography and Marine Services, Anchorage, pp. 451-540. This literature was collected as part of a metadatabase that represents one or more nearshore resources (from physical attributes such as water and air, to birds and mammals). Each entry is viewable in three formats, 1) a geospatial explicit format (ArcView 3.3) named GIS ArcView 3.3 Near GEM project, 2) a spreadsheet format (Excel 2002 and within ArcView) named GEM Nearshore Metadata Set, and 3) a ProCite database named GEM Nearshore Metadata References, which consists of references included in 1 and 2 above. The ProCite database record number corresponds to the Excel Procite Number. Reference Procite #838 for this literature's metadata. The metadata are maintained in digital format (ArcView 3.3, Excel 2002, and Procite) and are available from the Alaska Science Center, USGS in Anchorage, Alaska. For a detailed description of the three databases and the project, see \"Bodkin, J. L., and T. Dean. 2003. Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project G-030687), US Geological Survey, Alaska Science Center, Anchorage, Alaska.\" available on-line at http://www.evostc.state.ak.us/pdf/GEM_final_reports/030687.pdf.\n\nDeposited content: descriptive metadata only; underlying research data are not included.\n\nPurpose: Part of the design effort of the \"Alternative Sampling Designs for Nearshore Monitoring\" (Gulf Ecosystem Monitoring and Research Project). Provides a comprehensive historical perspective of locations and types of past studies conducted in the nearshore marine communities within Gulf of Alaska in the form of a geographical information system database.",
  "url": "https://zenodo.org/records/23566154",
